WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.579
Validation logic should be tested like any other important application behavior.

00:00:04.579 --> 00:00:09.466
Unit tests should confirm that valid inputs pass and invalid inputs fail.

00:00:09.466 --> 00:00:14.352
Negative tests are especially important because validation exists to reject bad data.

00:00:14.352 --> 00:00:18.832
Test missing fields, extra fields, malformed values, unexpected types, type-confused inputs,

00:00:18.832 --> 00:00:21.682
excessive length, invalid formats, and business-rule violations.

00:00:21.682 --> 00:00:23.718
Boundary tests make contracts concrete.

00:00:23.718 --> 00:00:28.605
If a string allows one hundred characters, test one hundred and one.

00:00:28.605 --> 00:00:33.898
If a number must be between one and ten, test zero, one, ten,

00:00:33.898 --> 00:00:34.713
and eleven.

00:00:34.713 --> 00:00:40.007
If an array must contain a limited number of items, test empty arrays,

00:00:40.007 --> 00:00:42.043
maximum length, and over-limit input.

00:00:42.043 --> 00:00:46.929
These tests catch common mistakes caused by off-by-one assumptions and incomplete schemas.

00:00:46.929 --> 00:00:51.408
API contract tests can confirm that endpoints enforce the documented schema.

00:00:51.408 --> 00:00:57.109
They are useful when multiple clients, services, or teams depend on the same API.

00:00:57.109 --> 00:01:02.403
Fuzzing at a high level can help discover parser behavior and unexpected crashes,

00:01:02.403 --> 00:01:06.068
especially around complex JSON structures, encodings, and nested inputs.

00:01:06.068 --> 00:01:08.511
Fuzzing does not replace clear requirements,

00:01:08.511 --> 00:01:12.176
but it can find cases developers did not anticipate.

00:01:12.176 --> 00:01:14.619
Invalid input should be logged safely.

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:18.284
Logging can help detect abuse and diagnose integration problems,

00:01:18.284 --> 00:01:22.356
but raw payloads may contain secrets, personal data, control characters,

00:01:22.356 --> 00:01:23.985
or intentionally noisy content.

00:01:23.985 --> 00:01:29.686
Test not only the happy path, but also how failures are reported, logged, rate-limited,

00:01:29.686 --> 00:01:31.722
and returned to the client.

00:01:31.722 --> 00:01:34.980
Predictable failure behavior is part of validation quality.
